<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Reichskommissariat Ostland</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1945 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Nazi German occupation regime of the Baltic states and western Belarus from 1941 to 1945, scene of some of the largest massacres of the Holocaust.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the invasion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1941, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> placed the occupied Baltic states and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> under a civil administration, Reichskommissariat Ostland, created in July 1941 under Alfred Rosenberg's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-ministry-for-the-occupied-eastern-territories">Ministry for the Occupied Eastern Territories</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinrich-lohse">Hinrich Lohse</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reich Commissioner</a>, with his seat in Riga.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ostland was divided into general commissariats for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> and White Ruthenia, each with local auxiliary administrations. The occupiers exploited labour and food and planned eventual German colonisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">The Holocaust</a> was carried out on a vast scale: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were murdered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumbula-massacre">Rumbula</a> near Riga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ponary-massacre">Ponary</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, the Ninth Fort in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maly-trostenets">Maly Trostenets</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Soviet offensives in 1944 retook most of the territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinrich-lohse">Lohse</a> fled. German forces trapped in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland-pocket">Courland Pocket</a> held out until May 1945. The region returned to Soviet rule as the Estonian, Latvian and Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republics and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byelorussian-ssr">Soviet Belarus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/reichskommissariat-ostland.jpg" alt="Reichskommissariat Ostland" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: German government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany_(1935%E2%80%931945).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1941 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hinrich Lohse</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran, Catholic and Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latvian, Lithuanian, Estonian, Belarusian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reich Commissariat Ostland, RKO</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1943&amp;focus=reichskommissariat-ostland" aria-label="Reichskommissariat Ostland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5200 245 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Reichskommissariat Ostland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5520 501L5521 437M5520 501L5520 501M5520 501L5526 548M5520 501L5300 622M5520 501L5521 437M5520 501L5520 501M5520 501L5526 556"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5521 437h0M5520 501h0M5526 548h0M5300 622h0M5521 437h0M5520 501h0M5526 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 501h0"/><path clas